    "acquired_at": "2026-11-17T15:01:47.090208+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-17T15:01:47.090208+00:00",
    "body": "Loftware agreed to buy Seagull Software, the maker of BarTender, a labeling and tracking platform used by more than 250,000 organizations. The deal would bring BarTender’s barcode printing and item-level tracking tools into the same business as Loftware’s packaging artwork and supplier labeling software .\nWhat’s Related\nLoftware, based in Portsmouth, New Hampshire, said the combined business would cover more of the work involved in identifying a product, from designing its packaging to printing its label and tracking it through the supply chain.\nSeagull, based in Redmond, Washington, makes software that automates the design and printing of labels, barcodes, and radio-frequency identification ( RFID ) tags. Seagull says customers use BarTender to handle more than 100 billion labels, tags, date codes and other identifiers each year.\nTracking starts when a label prints\nIn May, Seagull connected BarTender’s labeling software to its Track & Trace product. When a label prints, the software can automatically register the item for tracking and pass along details such as the item’s unique code, the time of printing, and its location. That saves workers from entering the same information into a tracking system by hand.\nBarTender can also exchange data with warehouse management and enterprise planning systems. A warehouse can use that connection to check whether outbound shipments match the quantities on a shipping order or pull current inventory data for a cycle count. Seagull added to its item-tracking business in 2024 when it combined with inventory software company Mojix.\nRELATED: Loftware Buys BL.INK to Boost QR Codes and Product Tracking\nHow Loftware handles supplier labels\nLoftware’s software helps companies manage labels for large product catalogs across multiple sites. Its Smartflow product handles packaging artwork reviews and approvals. In April, the company launched Loftware Connect, which lets a buyer publish labeling requirements and lets approved suppliers use those requirements to label shipments without accessing the buyer’s internal systems.\nStill waiting on timeline\nThe agreement follows Loftware’s 2021 combination with NiceLabel, another label design and printing software company. That deal brought NiceLabel under Loftware’s corporate brand while keeping the NiceLabel name on its products. The Seagull agreement would put NiceLabel and BarTender under the same owner.\nLoftware said it expects customers to be able to keep using their existing systems as the companies come together. The announcement gave no timeline for connecting the products and provided no details on future licensing or pricing for BarTender customers.",
